sentences.t:
Quando lo sopo mio Çid el de Bivar,
quel creçe conpaña, por que más valdrá,
apriessa cavalga, reçebir los sale;

sentences.l:
When it learned my Çid the-one of Bivar,
that-to-him grows company, whereby more he-will-be-worth,
in-haste he-rides, to-receive them he-goes-out;

sentences.t:
Se[e]d me[m]brados commo lo devedes far:
a la mañana, quando los gallos cantarán,
non vos tardedes, mandedes ensellar;

sentences.l:
Be mindful how it you-must do:
at the morning, when the cocks will-sing,
not yourselves delay, order to-saddle;

sentences.t:
“Ya señor glorioso, padre que en çielo estas[e],
fezist çielo e tierra, el terçero el mar[e];
fezist estrellas e luna y el sol pora escalentar[e];

sentences.l:
“O lord glorious, father who in heaven art,
thou-madest heaven and earth, the third the sea;
thou-madest stars and moon and the sun for to-warm;

sentences.t:
prisist encarnaçión en santa María madre,
en Belleem apareçist, commo f[o] tu veluntad[e];
pastores te glorifficaron, ouieron [te] a laudare,

sentences.l:
thou-tookest incarnation in holy Mary mother,
in Bethlehem thou-appearedst, as was thy will;
shepherds thee glorified, they-had [thee] to praise,

sentences.t:
tres reyes de Arabia te vinieron adorar[e],
Melchior e [C]aspar e Baltasar[e],
oro e tus e mirra te offreçieron [de] veluntad[e];

sentences.l:
three kings of Arabia thee came to-adore,
Melchior and [C]aspar and Baltasar,
gold and frankincense and myrrh to-thee they-offered [of] will;

sentences.t:
a los judios te dexeste prender; do dizen monte Calvarie
pusiéronte en cruz por nombre en Golgotá;
dos ladrones contigo, estos de señas partes,
el uno es en para[d]iso, ca el otro non entró allá;

sentences.l:
to the Jews thyself thou-letst take; where they-call mount Calvary
they-put-thee on cross by name in Golgotha;
two thieves with-thee, these of each-one sides,
the one is in paradise, but the other not entered there;

sentences.t:
en el monumento [oviste] a resuçit[ar],
fust a los infiernos, commo f[o] tu voluntad;
[cr]ebanteste las puertas, e saqueste los santos padres.

sentences.l:
in the tomb thou-[hadst] to rise-again,
thou-wentest to the hells, as was thy will;
thou-brokest the gates, and thou-tookest-out the holy fathers.

sentences.t:
El Çid a doña Ximena ívala abraçar;
doña Ximena al Çid la manol va besar,
llorando de los ojos, que non sabe qué se far.

sentences.l:
The Çid to doña Ximena went-her to-embrace;
doña Ximena to-the Çid the hand-to-him goes to-kiss,
weeping from the eyes, so-that not she-knows what herself to-do.

sentences.t:
E él a las niñas tornólas a catar:
“a Dios vos acomiendo e al Padre spirital;
agora nos partimos, Dios sabe el ajuntar.”

sentences.l:
And he to the little-girls turned-them to look:
“to God you I-commend and to-the Father spiritual;
now ourselves we-part, God knows the rejoining.”

sentences.t:
A tan grand sabor fabló Minaya Albar Fáñez:
“Çid, do son vuestros esfuerços? en buen[a] nasquiestes de madre;
pensemos de ir nuestra vía, esto sea de vagar.

sentences.l:
At so great relish spoke Minaya Albar Fáñez:
“Çid, where are your strengths? in a-good (hour) you-were-born of mother;
let-us-think of going our way, this let-it-be of leisure.

sentences.t:
Vino mio Çid yazer a Spinaz de Can;
grandes yentes sele acojen essa noch de todas partes.
Otro dia mañana pienssa de cavalgar.

sentences.l:
Came my Çid to-lie at Spinaz de Can;
great peoples themselves-to-him gather that night from all parts.
Other day morning he-thinks of riding.

sentences.t:
Ixiendos va de tierra el Campeador leal,
de siniestro Sant Estevan, una buena çipdad,
passó por Alcobiella que de Castiella fin es ya;

sentences.l:
Going-out-himself goes from land the Campeador loyal,
of left Sant Estevan, a good city,
he-passed by Alcobiella which of Castile end is already;
